# Root .dockerignore — the single ignore file for every image build in this
# repo (ai-service, backend, backend test, frontend), all of which use the
# repository root as their build context.
#
# WHY ONE FILE AND NOT PER-DOCKERFILE IGNORE FILES
# ------------------------------------------------
# BuildKit supports <dockerfile>.dockerignore (e.g. backend/Dockerfile.dockerignore),
# but such a file REPLACES this one for that build rather than merging with it.
# Four near-duplicate copies would drift the first time a rule is added here and
# forgotten there. They would only be worth that cost if one image needed a path
# another image must exclude, and after auditing all four builds none does — the
# exclusions below are safe for every one of them. So: one file, and the
# per-build requirements are documented inline where they are non-obvious.
#
# TWO KINDS OF RULE LIVE HERE, and it is worth knowing which is which.
# BuildKit works out what a build actually needs from its COPY instructions, so
# a rule only shrinks a real transfer when the excluded path sits under a
# directory some Dockerfile copies. Every Dockerfile here copies a subtree
# (backend/, frontend/, ai-service/) plus the two root manifests — none copies
# the repository root — so:
#   * Rules under ai-service/, backend/ and frontend/ are load-bearing today.
#     Measured: excluding the Piper voices and friends took the ai-service
#     context from 134.59MB to 824kB.
#   * Rules for root-level directories (mobile/, backups/, .venv, .git ...) do
#     not shrink today's transfers, because nothing copies the root. They are
#     kept deliberately anyway: they bound the tree BuildKit has to scan, and
#     they mean a future `COPY . .` — or a fifth Dockerfile — cannot silently
#     pull 2.4GB of Flutter build output into an image. Cheap insurance, not a
#     measured saving; do not delete them believing they are dead weight.
#
# NOTE: Docker anchors a bare pattern to the build context root; it does not
# match at every depth. Rules meant to apply inside subdirectories must use a
# "**/" prefix, and rules meant to apply at the root need the bare spelling —
# which is why several entries below are listed both ways.

.git
.github
.venv
**/.venv
node_modules
**/node_modules
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
# Both spellings, per the anchoring note above. There is a root-level
# .pytest_cache in this working tree whose ACL is unreadable even to the owner
# (`Get-Acl` fails on it), which makes any build that copies the repository
# root die with "error from sender: open .pytest_cache: Access is denied".
# No current Dockerfile copies the root, so no build hits it today — but it is
# exactly the kind of thing the root-level rules above exist to fence off.
.pytest_cache
**/.pytest_cache
**/npm-debug.log
**/*.log

# Dockerfiles are read directly by the build engine, not via COPY/ADD, so
# they never need to be present inside the built image.
Dockerfile
**/Dockerfile
Dockerfile.*
**/Dockerfile.*
.dockerignore

# The Flutter app — by far the largest thing in the working tree (~2.4GB, most
# of it mobile/build and mobile/android). No Dockerfile builds or serves it:
# backend, backend-test, frontend and ai-service between them copy only
# backend/, frontend/ and ai-service/, and nothing under those trees references
# mobile/. One rule, deliberately: the nested build/.dart_tool/platform
# directories are all below this and need no separate entries.
mobile/

# Not needed inside any image, and can be large or sensitive.
db
database
docs
# Local database dumps and consolidation snapshots (~114MB). The *.dump and
# *.backup rules below already covered the dump files themselves, but not the
# .sql/.json siblings sitting beside them.
backups

# IDE / agent metadata.
.claude
**/.idea
**/*.iml

# --- Below here the rules sit under a copied subtree, and so are the ones that
# --- actually shrink what gets sent.

# Data-collection/training tooling is not required in runtime images.
ai-service/data
ai-service/tests
# Benchmark harness and its generated result artifacts — dev tooling, never
# imported by chatbot.main or virtual_doctor at runtime.
ai-service/benchmarks

# Piper TTS voices (~121MB for the ar_JO + en_US pair) — the single biggest
# win in this file. These are DOWNLOADED artifacts, not source: they are
# gitignored, and virtual_doctor/voice/tts.py fetches them into this directory
# on first use if absent. Baking them in made image contents depend on whether
# the developer had ever run TTS locally — present on their machine, absent on
# CI and on a clean checkout. docker-compose bind-mounts this directory
# instead, so the download persists across rebuilds the same way
# medorbit_hf_cache persists the Whisper weights.
ai-service/virtual_doctor/assets/tts_voices
# Runtime output: generated PDF reports and the synthesized-speech wav cache
# (~7MB locally). report_generator.py and tts.py both create it on first use.
ai-service/virtual_doctor/generated

# Frontend unit tests. Verified needed by neither image: the nginx image serves
# a static root, and the only backend contract tests that read frontend source
# (backend/tests/profile-communication-ux.test.js, scripts/signal-coverage-report.js)
# reach into frontend/src and frontend/public only. They run on the host — CI's
# frontend-auth-contract job and the `npm run test:*-ui` scripts invoke node
# directly, not through a container. Excluding them also stops nginx serving
# test files at /tests/* on port 8080. NOTE: backend/tests is deliberately NOT
# excluded — backend/Dockerfile.test needs it.
frontend/tests

**/uploads
**/coverage
**/*.backup
**/*.dump
**/*.sql.bak

.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

**/*.md
tempData
